= A neural network computed by the page renderer =

'''What this page is.''' A plain MediaWiki file with no JavaScript and no program running when you read it. A character model (2 characters in, 12 ReLU hidden units, 5 output bits, 32 symbols; &#9251; = space) predicts the next character for every 2-character window of the text below. The text was taken from the title of a GitHub issue.

== Who computes what ==
* '''Written by the generator script''' (it only writes text into this file): the trained integer weights as literals, the embedding-vector numbers of the input characters as literals, and the input characters themselves after sanitizing (lowercase, vocabulary only).
* '''Computed by GitHub's renderer when you open this page''': every multiply-accumulate of both layers (<code>#expr</code>), every ReLU (<code>#ifeq</code> on a <code>#expr</code> comparison), every output sign test (<code>#expr</code> comparison) and the 5-bit decode to a character (<code>#switch</code>). The generator never evaluates the network to write a result into the page; no cell below holds a precomputed prediction.
* If the renderer did not compute, the prediction cells would show raw <code>&#123;&#123;#switch...</code> text instead of a letter.

Input after sanitizing: <b>&#104;&#101;&#108;&#108;&#111;&#9251;&#116;&#104;&#101;&#114;&#101;</b> (11 characters).

